  "final_text": "Both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't run the rule's query against the data, and its status is still \"pending\", so I don't know yet whether it fires correctly.\n\n**Fields:** the data view reads `kibana_sample_data_logs`, with `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request. The fields that matter most here are:\n- `response`, the HTTP status code. It's stored as a string, so I used its `.keyword` subfield.\n- `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` / `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url` and `agent`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-da3d7b55 Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests (single number)\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Responses by status code (pie chart)\n- Average bytes by file extension (bar chart)\n\nIt's at http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-da3d7b55-web-logs-overview-7aa993e7e335.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-da3d7b55 5xx spike\" (id `cc7b16b0-8387-41fc-a8d6-6dd007bbb477`) is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute and fires when more than 10 documents with a `response` of 5xx (matched by the regexp `5[0-9][0-9]`) arrive in a 5-minute window. It has no action attached, so when it fires it shows up as an alert in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone.",
